import { createServer } from "node:http";

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.method === "GET" && req.url === "/healthz") {
    const body = JSON.stringify({ status: "ok" });
    res.writeHead(200, {
      "Content-Type": "application/json; charset=utf-8",
      "Content-Length": Buffer.byteLength(body),
    });
    res.end(body);
    return;
  }
  res.writeHead(404, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
  res.end("Not Found");
});

server.listen(4000, "127.0.0.1");

import express from "express";

const app = express();

app.get("/healthz", (_req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: "ok" });
});

app.listen(4000, "127.0.0.1");

steps.body:
Nearly every API has one route that only answers: yes, I'm up. The tools that run your API in production, like the load balancers in the lesson on scaling out, call it every few seconds, and stop sending traffic to a copy that doesn't answer. It's also the simplest route there is, so it proves the whole setup works before you write anything real. Roombook's is `GET /healthz`. The z is a habit from Google's servers, and it makes the path unlikely to clash with a real resource.

## app.ts

Create `src/app.ts`:

```ts
import express from "express";

export function createApp() {
  const app = express();
  app.disable("x-powered-by");

  app.get("/healthz", (_req, res) => {
    res.json({ status: "ok" });
  });

  return app;
}
```

`express()` makes an app. By default, Express adds an `X-Powered-By: Express` header to every response, which tells the whole world which framework you run and helps attackers more than anyone, so `app.disable` turns it off from the start, ahead of the other security settings in the security lesson. `app.get` adds a route: GET requests for `/healthz` go to the handler, and `res.json` sends the object as JSON. The next lesson covers routes in full.

## server.ts

Create `src/server.ts`:

```ts
import { createApp } from "./app.ts";

const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 4000);
const host = process.env.HOST ?? "127.0.0.1";

const app = createApp();

app.listen(port, host, (error) => {
  if (error) throw error;
  console.log(`Roombook API listening on http://${host}:${port}`);
});
```

`process.env` holds the environment variables, always as text, so `Number` turns the port into a number. `??` falls back to 4000 when `PORT` isn't set. Many Node.js examples use port 3000, but Hack University itself listens on 3000 on your computer, so Roombook uses 4000 to stay out of its way.

`host` is the address to listen on. Recall from the web course that `127.0.0.1` is loopback: only programs on your own computer can connect, which is what you want while you build. When Roombook runs on a real server, in the lesson on deploying, `HOST` is set to `0.0.0.0` so other machines can reach it.

`app.listen` opens the port and calls the function when it's done. If it can't, Express 5 passes the error to that function, and `throw` stops the server with the message. The most common one is `Error: listen EADDRINUSE: address already in use 127.0.0.1:4000`: another program, often an earlier copy of this server in another terminal, already has the port. Stop that one with Ctrl+C, or pick another port.

## Run it

Start the server with `npm run dev`, as the first example shows. It keeps running, and holds the terminal, until you press Ctrl+C. Thanks to `--watch`, saving any file it uses restarts it, so you never restart it by hand.

To run it on another port for a moment, set the variable in front of the command, which beats the value in `.env`:

```bash
PORT=9090 npm run dev
```

In Windows PowerShell, write `$env:PORT=9090; npm run dev` instead.

## Ask it with curl

The Playground's terminal is busy running the server, so send requests from a second one: your computer's own terminal, as in the web course, like Terminal on a Mac. The second example sends the request. Then ask for a path Roombook doesn't have yet:

```bash
curl -i http://localhost:4000/v1/rooms
```

Express answers `404 Not Found` with a small HTML page that says `Cannot GET /v1/rooms`. An API should answer every error in JSON, and the middleware lesson and the lesson on validation and errors set that up.

## Check the types

Node.js never checks your types, so a mistake like `res.jsn` instead of `res.json` starts fine and only fails when a request reaches that line. `npm run typecheck` finds it first, as the third example shows. Run it before you commit, and the testing lesson makes the automated builds run it too.

